#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Prove each new bin/check assertion by breaking its target and confirming it fires.

An assertion never observed failing might be testing nothing (CLAUDE.md).
Each case breaks one file inside a DISPOSABLE COPY of the tree, runs that copy's
bin/check, and asserts the NAMED check appears in the failure list.

THE WORKING TREE IS NEVER WRITTEN TO, and the run says so from a content hash taken
before and after rather than from this sentence.

Why it is built this way — MEASURED 2026-08-04, all three numbers from this host:

    bin/check, one run ......................  1.8 s   (2026-08-04)
    bin/check, one run ......................  9.1 s   (2026-09-10, 441 cases)
    bin/prove, sequential and in place ...... 181 s
    bin/prove, parallel against copies ...... 60-88 s (4 workers, 4 cores; the
                                                       spread is machine load)

RE-MEASURED 2026-09-10, same host, and the figures above are STALE BY ~25x. `bin/check`
now takes 9.1 s rather than 1.8 s and CASES holds 441 entries, so a full run is
441 x 9.1 / 4 = ~17 min floor and ~33 min real. The 60-88 s line is kept rather than
overwritten because Core Principle 9b says a measurement carries the day it was taken:
it was true, and what it now records is how fast this suite grew. **The remedy is the
case filter, not a faster claim** — `bin/prove <substring>` runs only the cases whose
check name matches, which is ~14 s for the handful a single change actually adds.
    default harness Bash timeout ............ 120 s

The old design broke files in the real tree and restored them in a `finally`, so a
full run could not fit in a foreground call. It was therefore habitually backgrounded
— and a backgrounded process is killed by SIGTERM at session teardown, which does NOT
run `finally`. Measured both directions: SIGINT restored the file, SIGTERM left it
MUTATED with the temp backup orphaned, and nothing recorded which file it had been.
That is a 181-second window in which the tree carries a deliberately-sabotaged file
and `git commit -a` would commit it.

Copies fix the cause rather than the symptom: workers are independent, so the run is
parallel and fits in a foreground call, AND no signal at any moment can corrupt the
tree, because the tree is not the thing being written.

Note which of those two matters. 88 s clears the 120 s timeout on THIS host and that
margin is not large — a slower or busier machine can still push a run past it. That is
fine, and it is the point: backgrounding is now SAFE rather than merely unnecessary.
The correctness of a run no longer depends on how long it takes. If a host does need
to background it, pass a timeout and let the harness notify — never spawn a waiter
that polls, which is the habit that surfaced this whole defect.

The handler below survives for
the smaller job it still has — unwinding the TemporaryDirectory contexts so a kill
does not orphan them in /tmp. Never replace it with atexit: atexit handlers do not run
on a default-disposition SIGTERM either.

USAGE

    bin/prove                    every case (~30 min: 722 x 9.6 s of bin/check / 4 workers)
    bin/prove <substring>        the cases whose CHECK NAME contains it
    bin/prove --since <ref>      the cases whose TARGET FILE changed since <ref>

`--since` is the one to reach for after an edit. The name filter still asks you to know
which assertion your change touched; `--since HEAD` asks git instead and is exact,
because every case names its target file. MEASURED 2026-09-10: 18 s against 30 minutes.

THREE VERDICTS, NOT TWO, and the third is why the other two can be trusted:

    PROVEN     the mutation landed and the NAMED check fired
    VACUOUS    the mutation landed, check ran, and the assertion STILL PASSED.
               A finding about the ASSERTION — it is testing nothing.
    BAD-CASE   the mutation wrecked the file, so check failed on OTHER assertions and
               the named one never ran. A finding about the CASE. The assertion is
               unproven, not disproven.

`VACUOUS` and `BAD-CASE` were one label until 2026-09-10, and `len(fails)` had told them
apart the whole time. Four cases in one session were called VACUOUS when the mutation was
at fault, each costing a hand re-derivation of the same conclusion — and worse, a real
vacuous assertion hides among them, because the label that should stop a release reads
identically to the one that means "retarget your case". The split found a mis-filed case
within a minute of shipping: it had been filed under two wrong check names, reported
PROVEN under the first only because the assertion that reads its literal did not exist yet.
"""
import concurrent.futures, hashlib, os, pathlib, shutil, signal, subprocess, sys, tempfile
import threading

ROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent   # not hardcoded: a clone is a valid checkout

# `--help` PRINTS THE USAGE AND EXITS, and it is placed HERE — above the baseline check
# and every case — deliberately. Without it the flag falls through (it starts with `-`, so
# it is not a name filter) and the tool silently begins a THIRTY-MINUTE full run. Measured
# by doing it: asking this tool how to use it ran it twice. A reader asking how something
# works must never be answered by running it.
if "--help" in sys.argv[1:] or "-h" in sys.argv[1:]:
    print(__doc__[__doc__.index("USAGE"):].rstrip())
    sys.exit(0)

IGNORED_DIRS = {".git", "node_modules", "__pycache__", ".venv"}
IGNORE = shutil.ignore_patterns(*IGNORED_DIRS)

# One worker per core. bin/check is CPU-bound Python, and the work each worker does
# is a subprocess, so threads are enough — the GIL is released across the wait.
#
# `WF_PROVE_WORKERS` overrides it. Each worker holds a full copy of the tree plus a
# `bin/check` subprocess, so the run's peak footprint scales with the worker count,
# and core count is the wrong input for that on a host whose cores are already busy.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-08, and the first reading of it was WRONG — recorded here because
# the wrong reading is the instructive half. A 4-worker run on a 4-core host carrying
# two other agent sessions (load average 13.5) was killed partway through 556 cases
# after 35 minutes, and the kill was attributed to memory pressure. It was not: `free`
# reported 13 GiB of 30 GiB available, and the 2-worker retry died after FOUR cases,
# which no footprint argument explains. The killer was the AGENT HARNESS's background
# -task supervisor, which reaps a long-running background command on its own signal,
# not the kernel's. Fewer workers does not help with that; running the suite outside
# the supervisor's reach does (`setsid`), or running it in shards.
#
# The knob is kept anyway, because the footprint argument is still true for a genuinely
# small host — but it is NOT the remedy for a harness kill, and reaching for it there
# costs another 35 minutes to learn nothing.
WORKERS = max(1, os.cpu_count() or 2)
try:
    _w = int(os.environ.get("WF_PROVE_WORKERS", "") or 0)
    if _w > 0:
        WORKERS = _w
except ValueError:
    # A garbage value falls back to the core count rather than refusing the run: a
    # typo in an env var must not be the thing that stops a suite from being run.
    pass

STOP = threading.Event()


def _restore_and_die(signum, _frame):
    raise KeyboardInterrupt(f"terminated by signal {signum}")


for _sig in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP):
    signal.signal(_sig, _restore_and_die)

